    # THE WATCHTOWER SWITCH (SPEC.md §spec:container-updates).
    #
    # scripts/render-ignition.sh appends this fragment to butane/kantainer.bu.tmpl
    # when, and only when, the operator set KANTAINER_WATCHTOWER_ENABLED=true. A
    # machine that did not ask for Watchtower carries no trace of it in its
    # Ignition config at all - exactly as a wired machine carries no wireless
    # profile.
    #
    # It is a file rather than an enabled unit because at this moment the machine
    # is still stock Fedora CoreOS: kantainer-watchtower.service does not exist
    # yet and will not until the rebase in §spec:installer-media has run.
    # `systemctl enable` has nothing to enable. The unit ships already enabled
    # inside the image and reads this path as its ConditionPathExists, so the
    # file is the whole of the switch.
    #
    # THE CONTENTS ARE NOT READ. Existence is the signal - nothing parses this
    # file, so a machine where an operator wrote `false` into it by hand would
    # still run Watchtower. The line inside says so to whoever opens it, which is
    # the only place that warning can usefully live.
    #
    # /etc is persistent and carried forward across OS updates and rollbacks, so
    # this outlives every later image.
    - path: /etc/kantainer/watchtower-enabled
      mode: 0644
      user:
        id: 0
      group:
        id: 0
      contents:
        inline: |
          # This file existing is what starts kantainer-watchtower.service.
          # Nothing reads what is written here. To switch Watchtower off:
          #
          #     sudo rm /etc/kantainer/watchtower-enabled
          #     sudo systemctl stop kantainer-watchtower
